Send the nub axis as zero inside the gamepad deadzone

handle() forwarded a nub axis to the pyraInput Gamepad only when the value was
outside nubs.deadzone, and sent nothing otherwise. A nub released quickly
jumps from a large value straight into the deadzone, so the gamepad kept the
last large value: the stick stayed off centre until the nub was moved again.
Send 0 inside the deadzone, which is what the check was meant to do. The fix
is mati's, posted on the Pyra forum in September 2025 and tested there on
bookworm.

Without a rebuild, nubs.deadzone = -1 avoids it: every value is forwarded,
including the resting one.

Against funkeymonkey-pyrainput 0.1.19 (applies with or without 0001).
Apply with: patch -p1 < this-file

